From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 08:40:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Keep the caller's pose_cfg out of Keras attribute tracking

BaseKerasPoseNet stored the config with a plain

    self.cfg = cfg

and the class is a tf.keras.Model, so that assignment went through
tf.keras.Model.__setattr__.  That replaces any list or dict it is handed with
a ListWrapper or DictWrapper so the contents can be checkpointed, and for a
nested structure it does so *in place*.  Building a Keras pose net therefore
handed the caller back its own pose_cfg dict with the lists inside it
replaced by trackable objects.

Reading tests/data/pose_cfg.yaml and building either Keras net leaves six of
its top-level values wrapped -- all_joints, all_joints_names, multi_step,
partaffinityfield_graph, motion_blur and mean_pixel -- and all_joints, a list
of lists, is wrapped recursively.

Nothing raises at that point, which is why it was first seen as harmless in a
training loop.  The damage happens later: DeepLabCut writes these dicts back
to disk with yaml.dump in several places --
auxiliaryfunctions.write_plainconfig, core.config.write_config, and the
pose_cfg round trip in create_training_dataset -- and a ListWrapper does not
dump as a YAML list.  Dumping that same config produces 90 lines carrying

    !!python/object/apply:tensorflow.python.trackable.data_structures.ListWrapper

so the first thing that re-saved a config after a Keras net had been built
would have written a pose_cfg.yaml or config.yaml full of Python object tags.
That is silent, persistent and user visible, and it survives the process that
caused it.

Fix: assign cfg with object.__setattr__, wrapped in a set_untracked() helper
that carries the explanation, so Keras never sees the assignment.  Layers and
weights still go through normal attribute assignment and stay tracked, so
checkpointing, trainable_variables and slim_weight_map() are unaffected.

Deliberately not a deepcopy.  Keeping the identity of the dict is what
BasePoseNet does, and PoseMultiNet relies on it: its __init__ writes
cfg["multi_stage"] = multi_stage and "resnet" in net_type back into the
caller's dict.  A copy would have silently dropped that write-through and made
the Keras net diverge from the tf_slim one.  There is a test for that
specifically, so a future deepcopy "fix" fails instead of changing behaviour
quietly.

set_untracked() is also the answer for the backbones still being ported: a
Keras backbone that keeps its own reference to cfg, or to anything else the
caller owns, has to use it too.  Both the helper and the BaseKerasPoseNet
docstring say so.

Verified by round-tripping a real config: dump tests/data/pose_cfg.yaml,
build a net, dump again, and the two are byte-identical with no !!python tags,
both through yaml.dump directly and through DeepLabCut's own
write_plainconfig / read_plainconfig.  Eleven new assertions in
tests/test_nnets_keras.py cover it, and all eleven fail without this change.
The last of them is parametrised over net_type rather than over the nets
registered today, so a backbone added later is covered without editing the
test; it skips with the factory's own message while there is no
implementation.

Full TensorFlow test suite: 2461 passed, 7 skipped, 5 xfailed.
---
 .../pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/base.py  |  35 +++++-
 tests/test_nnets_keras.py                     | 100 ++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 134 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/base.py b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/base.py
index ce142052..b82b0af2 100644
--- a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/base.py
+++ b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/base.py
@@ -235,6 +235,31 @@ class BasePoseNet(metaclass=abc.ABCMeta):
         return inputs - mean
 
 
+def set_untracked(obj, name, value):
+    """Assign an attribute on a Keras object without Keras tracking it.
+
+    ``tf.keras.Model.__setattr__`` replaces any list or dict it is handed with
+    a ``ListWrapper``/``DictWrapper`` so the objects inside can be checkpointed,
+    and for a nested structure it does that **in place**.  A pose net that
+    simply did ``self.cfg = cfg`` would therefore hand the caller back its own
+    ``pose_cfg`` dict with every list and sub-dict replaced by a trackable
+    wrapper.  Nothing fails at that point, but DeepLabCut writes those dicts
+    back out with ``yaml.dump`` -- ``auxiliaryfunctions.write_plainconfig``,
+    ``write_config``, the ``pose_cfg`` round trip in
+    ``create_training_dataset`` -- and a wrapper does not dump as a plain YAML
+    list; it dumps as a ``!!python/object/apply`` tag, which corrupts the
+    user's ``pose_cfg.yaml`` or ``config.yaml`` permanently.
+
+    Bypassing the tracking also keeps the attribute the very same object the
+    caller passed, which is what ``BasePoseNet`` does and what
+    ``PoseMultiNet`` relies on when it writes ``cfg["multi_stage"]`` back.
+
+    Use this for anything a caller owns.  Layers and weights must NOT go
+    through it: those have to stay tracked.
+    """
+    object.__setattr__(obj, name, value)
+
+
 def ensure_eager_execution():
     """Make sure eager execution is on, and say so loudly when it cannot be.
 
@@ -279,12 +304,20 @@ class BaseKerasPoseNet(tf.keras.Model, BasePoseNet):
     There is no ``Session``, no placeholder, no ``variable_scope`` and no
     ``arg_scope``: the ``tf_slim`` scope names survive only as strings, in the
     ``slim_scope`` of each layer.
+
+    ``cfg`` is stored with :func:`set_untracked`, so building a net leaves the
+    caller's ``pose_cfg`` dict exactly as it was.  A subclass or a backbone
+    that keeps its own reference to ``cfg``, or to anything else the caller
+    owns, has to do the same.
     """
 
     def __init__(self, cfg, **kwargs):
         ensure_eager_execution()
         tf.keras.Model.__init__(self, **kwargs)
-        self.cfg = cfg
+        # The caller owns cfg; keep it out of Keras's attribute tracking, which
+        # would otherwise rewrite its lists and dicts in place.  See
+        # set_untracked().
+        set_untracked(self, "cfg", cfg)
         self._heads = {}
 
     # -- layer construction helpers ----------------------------------------
diff --git a/tests/test_nnets_keras.py b/tests/test_nnets_keras.py
index 92726926..00cd2317 100644
--- a/tests/test_nnets_keras.py
+++ b/tests/test_nnets_keras.py
@@ -332,3 +332,103 @@ def test_a_few_sgd_steps_lower_the_loss():
         optimizer.apply_gradients(zip(tape.gradient(loss, model.trainable_variables), model.trainable_variables))
         losses.append(float(loss))
     assert losses[-1] < losses[0]
+
+
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# the caller's config dict
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+def _trackable_values(cfg):
+    """Values Keras has replaced with one of its tracking wrappers."""
+    found = {}
+    for key, value in cfg.items():
+        name = type(value).__name__
+        if name in ("ListWrapper", "_DictWrapper", "_ListWrapper", "DictWrapper", "_TupleWrapper"):
+            found[key] = name
+    return found
+
+
+@pytest.mark.parametrize("name,cfg,n_slim_variables", CONFIGS, ids=[c[0] for c in CONFIGS])
+def test_building_a_net_leaves_the_callers_config_alone(name, cfg, n_slim_variables):
+    """A pose net must not rewrite the dict it was handed.
+
+    tf.keras.Model.__setattr__ replaces lists and dicts with ListWrapper /
+    DictWrapper in place, so a plain ``self.cfg = cfg`` would hand the caller
+    back a pose_cfg whose lists are trackable objects.  yaml.dump writes those
+    as ``!!python/object/apply`` tags, and DeepLabCut saves these dicts back to
+    disk in several places, so the user's pose_cfg.yaml would be corrupted.
+    """
+    yaml = pytest.importorskip("yaml")
+    cfg = dict(cfg)
+    before = yaml.dump(cfg, default_flow_style=False)
+
+    model = PoseNetFactory.create(cfg, engine=KERAS_ENGINE)
+
+    after = yaml.dump(cfg, default_flow_style=False)
+    assert _trackable_values(cfg) == {}
+    assert "!!python" not in after
+    assert after == before
+    # and the net keeps the caller's dict itself, as BasePoseNet does
+    assert model.cfg is cfg
+
+
+def test_config_survives_deeplabcuts_own_yaml_writers(tmp_path):
+    """The real corruption path: build a net, then let DeepLabCut save the config."""
+    yaml = pytest.importorskip("yaml")
+    from deeplabcut.utils.auxiliaryfunctions import read_plainconfig, write_plainconfig
+
+    cfg = multi_animal_cfg(stride=4, multi_stage=True)
+    cfg["all_joints"] = [[i] for i in range(cfg["num_joints"])]
+    cfg["multi_step"] = [[0.005, 10000], [0.02, 430000]]
+
+    reference = tmp_path / "pose_cfg_before.yaml"
+    write_plainconfig(str(reference), cfg)
+
+    PoseNetFactory.create(cfg, engine=KERAS_ENGINE)
+
+    written = tmp_path / "pose_cfg_after.yaml"
+    write_plainconfig(str(written), cfg)
+
+    assert "!!python" not in written.read_text()
+    assert written.read_text() == reference.read_text()
+    assert read_plainconfig(str(written)) == read_plainconfig(str(reference))
+    del yaml
+
+
+def test_multi_stage_is_written_back_like_the_tf_slim_net():
+    """PoseMultiNet edits cfg["multi_stage"] in place; the Keras net must too."""
+    from deeplabcut.pose_estimation_tensorflow.nnets.multi import PoseMultiNet
+
+    for net_type, expected in (("resnet_50", True), ("mobilenet_v2_1.0", False)):
+        slim_cfg = multi_animal_cfg(stride=4, multi_stage=True, net_type=net_type)
+        PoseMultiNet(slim_cfg)
+        assert slim_cfg["multi_stage"] is expected
+
+    keras_cfg = multi_animal_cfg(stride=4, multi_stage=True, net_type="resnet_50")
+    PoseNetFactory.create(keras_cfg, engine=KERAS_ENGINE)
+    assert keras_cfg["multi_stage"] is True
+
+
+@pytest.mark.parametrize(
+    "net_type",
+    ["resnet_50", "resnet_101", "mobilenet_v2_1.0", "mobilenet_v2_0.35", "efficientnet-b0"],
+)
+@pytest.mark.parametrize("stride", [8, 4])
+def test_no_registered_keras_net_touches_the_callers_config(net_type, stride):
+    """Same guarantee, for every backbone that has a Keras implementation.
+
+    Parametrised over net_types rather than over the nets registered today, so
+    a backbone added later is covered without editing this test.
+    """
+    yaml = pytest.importorskip("yaml")
+    cfg = multi_animal_cfg(stride=stride, multi_stage=stride < 8, net_type=net_type)
+    before = yaml.dump(cfg, default_flow_style=False)
+    try:
+        model = PoseNetFactory.create(cfg, engine=KERAS_ENGINE)
+    except ValueError as err:
+        pytest.skip(f"no Keras implementation yet: {err}")
+    cfg["multi_stage"] = "resnet" in net_type and stride < 8  # the one edit that is by design
+    assert _trackable_values(cfg) == {}
+    after = yaml.dump(cfg, default_flow_style=False)
+    assert "!!python" not in after
+    assert after == before
+    assert model.cfg is cfg
